# .env.kdf.example, the credentials kriegerdataforge-portfolio needs for local work (env standard, cicd ADR D-030)
#
# Copy this file to .env.kdf, then uncomment each line you use and fill it in. Every line starts commented out, so a
# copy overrides nothing until a person fills it. .env.kdf holds every credential that works beyond this machine. git
# ignores it and every AI session is refused it, so a person fills it in and no model reads it. Never commit it, and
# never paste a value from it anywhere. Values that work only on this machine go in .env.local, see
# .env.local.example.

# ─── Private GitHub repo, kdf-fmt over pip ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# A fine grained GitHub token, Contents read only, on Needless2Say/kriegerdataforge-fmt, never a classic token. It
# starts with github_pat_. make ci-style installs the private kdf-fmt formatter with it. An exported GH_PACKAGES_PAT
# wins over this line. Left commented, git uses its own credentials.
# GH_PACKAGES_PAT=

# ─── EmailJS, the KDF contact form ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Once the KriegerDataForge business email is ready.
#
# 1. Sign up at https://www.emailjs.com, the free tier sends 200 emails a month.
# 2. Add an email service, Dashboard, Email Services, Add New Service. Connect the KDF business email, and copy the
#    Service ID.
# 3. Create a template, Dashboard, Email Templates, Create New Template. ContactForm.tsx sends these variables.
#      {{from_name}}   the sender's name
#      {{reply_to}}    the sender's email, set it as Reply-To in the template settings
#      {{subject}}     the message subject
#      {{message}}     the message body
#    Set "To Email" to the KDF business email in the template, and copy the Template ID.
# 4. Get the public key, Dashboard, Account, General, Public Key.
# 5. Uncomment the three lines below and fill them in. For the deploy, add the same three as the repo's Actions
#    secrets, NEXT_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_SERVICE_ID, NEXT_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_TEMPLATE_ID and NEXT_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_PUBLIC_KEY.
#
# The keys are public by design, the static export inlines them into the shipped bundle, so the controls that stop
# abuse live in the EmailJS dashboard, Allowed Origins and rate limiting.
#
# Next.js reads .env.local and never .env.kdf, so the Makefile exports these three for make build, and compose hands
# .env.kdf to the dev container. Left commented, the site builds and runs, and only the contact form's send stays
# inert.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_SERVICE_ID=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_TEMPLATE_ID=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_PUBLIC_KEY=
